# Preserve source topology, UVs, materials, textures, pivots, rigs, animations,…

Preserve source topology, UVs, materials, textures, pivots, rigs, animations,
placements, and transforms without artistic edits.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Preserve source topology, UVs, materials, textures, pivots, rigs,
  animations, placements, and transforms without artistic edits.

## Dependencies

No explicit task dependency was recorded in the legacy TODO.

## Evidence

World-analysis mesh loading now uses the same strict authored-topology contract
as ordinary FBX mesh loading. Triangle-strip stitching degenerates remain a
deterministic topology conversion performed by strip triangulation, while an
authored triangle-list record with repeated vertex indices fails closed instead
of being silently deleted. A focused regression proves analysis no longer
repairs a malformed triangle list; the complete FBX package, strict FBX Clippy,
and all 54 world-level tests pass. The legacy world-catalog discard-count field
is retained for schema compatibility and is now zero for render meshes.

Intersect collision meshes now follow the same fail-closed rule. Their decoded
format is an explicit count-prefixed triangle index array with no strip/stitch
selector, so repeated-index source triangles are no longer deleted before mesh
construction. A focused collision regression proves the exact malformed source
topology is rejected, and the legacy collision discard count remains zero for
catalog compatibility.

World mesh publication now also preserves source mesh grouping itself. Spatial
distance between disconnected components no longer triggers vertex compaction,
primitive regrouping, or generated independent-object identities. Near and
distant synthetic meshes remain byte-equivalent at the typed mesh boundary,
and the position/UV preservation regression remains green.

Rigid-prop rest-transform baking now applies the inverse-transpose affine basis
to authored normals instead of reusing the position basis. Non-uniform authored
joint scale therefore preserves surface orientation while positions still use
the exact source rest transform; a focused regression proves a diagonal normal
under 2x X scale resolves to the mathematically correct transformed direction.

Skeletal PTRN binding now fails closed when a bound source group carries a
transform property other than the explicitly preserved `TRAN` and `ROT_`
channels. Unsupported authored transform data can no longer disappear from the
export silently; unmatched helper groups remain retained as ignored evidence.

Skeletal animation rest defaults now decompose the authored local matrix before
quaternion recovery, removing scale from the rotation basis while retaining the
exact decomposed translation. A 90-degree Z rest rotation with 2x authored X
scale now recovers the original rotation instead of a scale-distorted angle.

Bound skeletal animation keys now must remain inside the declared clip frame
range. Authored keys beyond the last exported frame fail closed instead of being
silently truncated while interpolation samples only the shorter clip duration.

Skeletal PTRN loading now also models decoder-produced top-level loose channels
and legacy animation extras explicitly. Non-empty payloads fail closed instead
of being silently discarded by Serde while grouped skeletal tracks are exported.

Skeleton loading now rejects joint-control semantics that the current FBX bone
contract cannot represent: nonzero DOF/free-axis/axis selectors and decoded
joint metadata. These authored rig controls can no longer be flattened silently
into an unconstrained parent/child hierarchy.

Whole-character loading now retains decoded composite-effect presence through
the shared binding parser and rejects non-empty effects because the current FBX
character contract cannot preserve their joint/translucency/sort semantics.
Explicit selected-rigid-prop export may still prune unrelated effect branches.

Primitive-group normal attachment now preserves already validated authored
triangle winding instead of swapping the second and third index when geometric
facing disagrees with the summed source vertex normals. Source topology and
source normals therefore remain independent evidence through the FBX domain;
the declared FBX export-root transform remains the orientation conversion
boundary.

A normalized-corpus audit found the removed repair would have changed 327 of
190,162 orientable mesh triangles across 64 primitive groups and 2,741 of
268,870 orientable skin triangles across 211 groups. These are input-evidence
counts, not a claim that every audited component is published. The complete FBX
package and all 1,006 pipeline library tests pass with strict FBX and pipeline
Clippy clean.

Decoded P3D triangle-list groups now require exact triples instead of allowing a
four-index partial list to fall through the generic quad triangulation helper.
Both mesh and skin adapters reject that malformed source before mesh or skin
binding continues, while explicit non-P3D quad callers retain their separate
domain helper contract. The normalized corpus contains 3,055 mesh and 198 skin
`prim_type=0` groups; every one has an index count divisible by three. Focused
red/green regressions cover both adapters and the full FBX and pipeline suites
remain green.

Decoded mesh and skin UV intake now has focused evidence for the existing
fail-closed single-channel contract. Both adapters reject a populated nonzero UV
channel instead of choosing one channel and dropping the rest; they also reject
duplicate channel-zero evidence. The normalized corpus contains 18,148 mesh
primitive groups and 1,462 skin primitive groups. All 19,610 groups carry
exactly one non-empty channel zero, with no nonzero or duplicate channel
identity observed.

Decoded P3D mesh `RENDERSTATUS` is now interpreted according to the schema's
`CastShadow` field instead of being discarded. `MeshAsset` retains the optional
boolean source flag, binary FBX mesh nodes publish declared values as
`SHAR_P3D_CastShadow` source metadata. Values outside `0` or `1` fail
closed.

The normalized corpus contains 14,297 decoded meshes: 14,283 declare `1`, 12
declare `0`, and two omit the chunk. A focused binary regression verifies the
disabled value is encoded as `0`, not merely that the property name exists.

Packed-normal evidence is redundant rather than exclusive in the current
normalized corpus. All 3,642 mesh groups and 1,462 skin groups carrying packed
normals also carry same-length decoded ordinary-normal arrays; no packed-only
group was observed. Across the same 19,610 primitive groups, no decoded tangent,
binormal, or multi-colour field is present. The adapters' strict JSON shape
continues to reject unsupported fields rather than dropping them silently.

Deferred world billboard controller relationships now retain their exact BQG
animation member identity and source ordinal instead of losing the animation
edge when model FBX publication omits billboard runtime semantics. This is
relationship preservation only: the decoded BQG channel payload remains the
source authority and is not flattened into skeletal or static FBX animation.

A focused audit of the 138 linked BQG animations finds 134 at 30 fps and four at
24 fps; all are non-cyclic and have no loose channels or legacy animation
extras. Eight animations contain four named groups, while the other 130 contain
one group. Channel sequences split into 82 standard eight-channel records, 43
width/height-only records, eight four-group repetitions of the standard set,
and five extended records that also carry `ERNG`, `SRNG`, and `ORNG`. Those
source differences remain explicitly deferred for a later faithful billboard
animation representation rather than normalized away.

Deferred world billboards now preserve their source presentation payload
separately from the static inspection planes used by FBX review. Across the 284
current child quads, authored orientation modes are 254 `NOAX`, 19 `YAX`, eight
`AAX`, and three `LYAX`; cutoff modes are 279 `N0NE` and five `DBL`. All current
children enable perspective, while authored distance is zero for 276 children,
`0.65` for seven, and `-0.35` for one.

The current UV-offset range, source range, and edge range are zero for all 284
children, but the v4 catalog still retains those fields rather than inferring a
global default. Ten shader identities and 55 child identities occur across the
260 groups, and no audited presentation float is non-finite. Camera-facing,
cutoff, depth, and BQG animation execution remain deferred to a reviewed native
presentation adapter instead of being flattened into static or skeletal FBX.

Deferred billboard shader evidence now uses the same validated decoded-shader
contract as FBX material resolution while preserving the full source parameter
array. Schema/version, platform shader identity, translucency, vertex masks,
declared parameter count, canonical `TEX`, and exact decoded parameter values
remain available without translating them into runtime material semantics.

Across the current 260 deferred groups, the retained relationships reference ten
shader identities and nine canonical texture tokens. Their 271 shader
occurrences carry 7,017 parameter rows through the catalog boundary; nine of the
11 ambiguous relationship sets differ in non-texture shader payload while none
disagree on `TEX`. Native blend, depth, camera-facing, cutoff, and animation
execution therefore remain deferred instead of being inferred from one selected
shader occurrence.

Replaying the existing cross-package texture authority against the phase-three
index resolves 248 of the 260 deferred billboard texture references without
conflict. The remaining 12 are all level-03 `glow2.bmp` uses: their preferred
terrain scope contains two distinct normalized PNG payloads for the same source
identity. No local billboard shader candidate disagrees about the `TEX` token,
but that agreement does not identify one of the two physical texture payloads.

Both conflicting level-03 textures are independent top-level chunks, and the
terrain shader that names `glow2.bmp` is also an independent top-level chunk.
There is therefore no decoded parent/container edge supporting an occurrence
choice. Texture publication for those deferred billboard semantics remains open
until stronger source or native evidence can disambiguate the relationship.

This umbrella task remains open for broader topology, UV, material, texture,
pivot, rig, animation, placement, and transform evidence.
